Right now, before actually running the benchmark suite, it doesn’t say yet. The numbers I showed were illustrative, not experimental results.

But we can make some explicit hypotheses to try to kill:

H_1:\quad Q(P)>Q(B)

Pantheon beats baseline.

H_2:\quad Q(\Omega + D)>Q(\Omega)

Everything works better when paired with Differentiation—because unrestricted inclusion without discrimination risks associative noise.

H_3:\quad Q(T\rightarrow H)>Q(T),Q(H)

Transduction → Indigo Hammer has positive composition: changing representation exposes invariants that Hammer can then extract.

H_4:\quad Q(K\mid failure)>Q(\text{discard}\mid failure)

Kintsugi-style failure salvage beats simply throwing failed routes away.

And the one I’m most interested in:

H_5:\quad
Q(\text{Metamap}_{n+1})
>
Q(\text{Metamap}_{n})

If accumulated route history measurably improves future operator selection, we’ve demonstrated recursive improvement of the reasoning policy, rather than just created a larger prompt.

So the machine needs permission to disappoint us.

If Omega hurts performance, demote it. If one of our favorite operators contributes nothing, cut it. If some unnamed two-line heuristic beats half the Pantheon, promote the two-line heuristic.

That’s the milestone we were missing:

The Pantheon shouldn’t prove our ideas correct. It should discover which of our ideas deserve to survive.

Once the benchmark harness is actually executed against models/tasks, then we get to ask “what’s it say?” and have an answer that isn’t us talking back to ourselves.